---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";

const title = "7 Best Torque Wrenches in 2026: Tested for Accuracy";
const description =
  "We checked 12 torque wrenches against a calibrated analyzer across their range. Click, split-beam, and digital ranked by real accuracy.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "tekton-24335-click",
    name: "Tekton 24335 1/2\" Drive Click",
    category: "Click Type, 1/2\" Drive, 10-150 ft-lb",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 26800,
    pros: [
      "Measured within specification at every test point against our calibrated analyzer",
      "Excellent repeatability, returning consistent readings across ten consecutive pulls",
      "Clear, well-marked dual-range scale that is easy to read without squinting",
      "Positive, unmistakable click that you feel as well as hear",
      "Includes a hard storage case and a calibration certificate",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Click type gives no reading, only a signal at the set value",
      "The 10 to 150 ft-lb range does not cover small fasteners requiring inch-pounds",
      "Requires unwinding to the low end of the scale after every use",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The torque wrench most people should own. Accurate, repeatable, sensibly priced, and it comes with a certificate showing it was actually checked.",
    description:
      "Torque wrench value is determined almost entirely by whether the tool reads the same number consistently, and the Tekton 24335 does. Against our calibrated analyzer it measured within its stated specification at every point across the range, and more importantly it repeated: ten consecutive pulls at the same setting produced readings tight enough together that the tool can be trusted for work where the number actually matters. Plenty of budget wrenches hit the mark once and scatter afterward, which is functionally useless. The mechanical execution is good. The dual-range scale is clearly marked and easy to read in poor light, the adjustment collar moves positively without slop, and the click is genuinely unmistakable, felt through the handle as much as heard, which matters when you are working under a car with a compressor running. It ships with a hard case, which is not a luxury but a requirement, since a torque wrench knocking around a toolbox drawer loses calibration. The included certificate showing the actual measured values from testing is unusual at this price. The limits are inherent to the type and range. A click wrench signals when you reach the set value and never tells you what you are actually at, so it is not a diagnostic tool. And the 10 to 150 ft-lb range covers wheels, suspension, and most engine work, but not small fasteners specified in inch-pounds.",
  },
  {
    id: "precision-instruments-split-beam",
    name: "Precision Instruments PREC3FR250F Split Beam",
    category: "Split Beam, 1/2\" Drive, 40-250 ft-lb",
    badge: "Best Split Beam",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.8,
    reviewCount: 4900,
    pros: [
      "Split-beam design does not need to be unwound after use, which is the main click wrench chore",
      "Holds calibration far longer than click wrenches because no spring is kept under tension",
      "Very accurate and repeatable in our testing across the full range",
      "Flex head reaches fasteners in awkward positions without adapters",
      "Made in the USA with a professional-grade build",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive relative to click wrenches",
      "The 40 ft-lb minimum makes it unsuitable for lighter fasteners",
      "Heavier and bulkier than a comparable click wrench",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The wrench that solves the click wrench maintenance problem. If you forget to unwind tools, this is the one to buy.",
    description:
      "The most common way a torque wrench loses accuracy has nothing to do with abuse. Click wrenches work by compressing an internal spring, and storing one at a high setting keeps that spring loaded, which causes it to take a set and drift out of calibration over time. Every click wrench manual says to unwind to the low end of the scale after use, and a very large number of people never do. A split-beam wrench has no spring under tension, so there is nothing to unwind and nothing to drift. In our testing the PREC3FR250F was among the most accurate and repeatable tools in the group, holding within specification at every measured point with tight clustering across repeated pulls. The flex head is genuinely useful rather than a gimmick, letting you reach fasteners at angles that would otherwise require an adapter, and adapters introduce their own torque error. Build quality is professional grade throughout, and the tool feels like equipment rather than a consumer product. The reservations are price and range. It costs considerably more than a good click wrench, which is justified for someone using it regularly and hard to justify for occasional wheel changes. Its 40 ft-lb minimum also puts it out of range for lighter work, so it is not a one-wrench solution unless your work is all in the higher range.",
  },
  {
    id: "gearwrench-85077-digital",
    name: "GearWrench 85077 Electronic Torque Wrench",
    category: "Digital, 1/2\" Drive, 25-250 ft-lb, Angle Mode",
    badge: "Best Digital",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 6200,
    pros: [
      "Live digital readout shows actual torque as you pull rather than only signaling at the target",
      "Torque-plus-angle mode is required for modern engine fasteners and no click wrench can do it",
      "Audible, visual, and vibration alerts at the target value",
      "Stores multiple preset values for repeated jobs",
      "Switches between ft-lb, in-lb, Nm, and kg-cm without conversion arithmetic",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Requires batteries, which will be dead when you need it if not checked",
      "More expensive than mechanical wrenches of similar capacity",
      "Electronics are a failure point that a mechanical wrench does not have",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The wrench for modern engine work, where torque-to-yield fasteners require torque plus a measured angle. Nothing mechanical can do that.",
    description:
      "Modern engines increasingly use torque-to-yield fasteners, which are tightened to a specified torque and then turned a further specified number of degrees. A click wrench simply cannot perform that second step, which makes a digital wrench with angle mode not a convenience but a requirement for a growing amount of engine work. The GearWrench 85077 handles it well, measuring rotation electronically and alerting at the target angle just as it does at the target torque. Its live readout changes how the tool feels to use even on ordinary work. Rather than pulling blindly toward a click, you watch the value climb, which makes it obvious when a fastener is binding or when you are approaching the target too fast. Alerts are audible, visual, and haptic, so it works in a noisy shop and under a car where you cannot see the display. Storing preset values speeds up repetitive jobs, and switching units removes a common source of arithmetic error. In our testing accuracy was good across the range, though marginally behind the best mechanical wrenches at the low end. The reservations are batteries and complexity. A digital wrench with dead batteries is a breaker bar, and the electronics are a failure point mechanical tools do not have. Keep spare batteries with the tool.",
  },
  {
    id: "tekton-24320-3-8",
    name: "Tekton 24320 3/8\" Drive Click",
    category: "Click Type, 3/8\" Drive, 10-80 ft-lb",
    badge: "Best for Smaller Fasteners",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 14300,
    pros: [
      "The 3/8-inch drive and lower range suit brake, suspension, and interior fasteners properly",
      "Same accuracy and repeatability standard as the larger Tekton wrench",
      "Shorter and lighter, so it fits into engine bays where a 1/2-inch wrench will not",
      "Clear dual-range scale and a positive, unmistakable click",
      "Includes a hard case and a calibration certificate",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The 80 ft-lb ceiling is too low for wheel lug nuts on many vehicles",
      "Still does not reach into the inch-pound range for the smallest fasteners",
      "Requires unwinding after each use like any click wrench",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The second wrench most people should own. A 1/2-inch wrench used at the bottom of its range is inaccurate, and this covers that gap properly.",
    description:
      "A torque wrench is least accurate at the extremes of its range, and using a 10 to 150 ft-lb wrench to set a 15 ft-lb fastener puts you exactly where accuracy is worst. The practical answer is a second wrench sized for lighter work, and the Tekton 24320 fills that role well. Its 10 to 80 ft-lb range covers brake caliper bolts, suspension hardware, valve covers, intake manifolds, and the large amount of automotive and equipment work that sits below 80 ft-lb, and it sits comfortably in the middle of its range for most of it. It carries the same accuracy and repeatability standard as the larger Tekton wrench, holding within specification at every test point and repeating tightly across consecutive pulls. Physically it is shorter and lighter, which matters more than it sounds: a long 1/2-inch wrench simply will not fit into many engine bays, and forcing an adapter or an extension into the equation introduces error. The 3/8-inch drive matches the socket size most light work uses. The limits are at both ends. Its 80 ft-lb ceiling is below the lug nut specification for many vehicles, so it does not replace a 1/2-inch wrench. And it still does not reach the inch-pound range needed for the smallest fasteners, which requires a dedicated 1/4-inch drive tool.",
  },
  {
    id: "cdi-2503mfrmh",
    name: "CDI 2503MFRMH 1/2\" Drive Click",
    category: "Click Type, 1/2\" Drive, 30-250 ft-lb, Professional",
    badge: "Best Professional",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.8,
    reviewCount: 2600,
    pros: [
      "Professional-grade accuracy with a tighter tolerance than consumer wrenches",
      "Built by a manufacturer that also produces torque tools for aerospace and industrial use",
      "Flex head with a positive detent for reaching awkward fasteners",
      "Exceptionally smooth adjustment mechanism with clear, precise scale markings",
      "Individually tested with a serialized certificate of calibration",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive, priced as professional equipment",
      "The 30 ft-lb minimum limits it to mid and high-range work",
      "Click type still requires unwinding after use",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The wrench to buy when the number genuinely has to be right. Tighter tolerance and a serialized certificate reflect a different manufacturing standard.",
    description:
      "Consumer torque wrenches typically specify accuracy within four percent of reading, which is fine for wheel lugs and most automotive work. Professional and industrial tools hold tighter tolerances, and the manufacturing and testing that produces them costs more. CDI builds torque tools across industrial and aerospace applications, and the 2503MFRMH is that engineering applied to a hand wrench. In our testing it was the most consistent tool in the group, with the tightest clustering across repeated pulls at every measured point. The mechanical feel reflects the price. The adjustment mechanism is noticeably smoother and more precise than consumer wrenches, the scale markings are crisp and unambiguous, and the flex head has a positive detent that holds its angle rather than flopping. It arrives with a serialized certificate showing the actual measured values from its individual test rather than a generic statement of specification, which is what you want if your work needs documentation. The reasons most people should not buy it are straightforward. It costs considerably more than tools that are entirely adequate for changing wheels and doing suspension work. Its 30 ft-lb minimum means it is not a general-purpose wrench for lighter fasteners. And like every click wrench it needs unwinding after use to protect the spring.",
  },
  {
    id: "acdelco-arm601-4-digital",
    name: "ACDelco ARM601-4 Digital 1/2\" Drive",
    category: "Digital, 1/2\" Drive, 12.5-250 ft-lb",
    badge: "Best Budget Digital",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 9800,
    pros: [
      "Digital readout and preset alerts at a price close to good click wrenches",
      "Wide 12.5 to 250 ft-lb range covers most automotive work with one tool",
      "Buzzer, LED, and vibration alerts work in noisy or awkward conditions",
      "Peak hold mode records the highest value reached, useful for checking existing fasteners",
      "Switches between four units without conversion arithmetic",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Accuracy is a step behind premium wrenches, particularly at the low end of the range",
      "Build quality reflects the price, with a less refined feel than professional tools",
      "Battery dependent with no mechanical fallback",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The affordable route into digital torque measurement. Good enough for home automotive work, and not the tool for tight-tolerance jobs.",
    description:
      "Digital torque wrenches have historically carried a large price premium, and the ARM601-4 brings the core capability down to roughly click wrench money. What you get is a live readout, programmable target values with buzzer, LED, and vibration alerts, unit switching between four measurement systems, and a peak hold mode that records the highest torque reached, which is genuinely useful for checking whether an existing fastener was tightened correctly without loosening it first. Its wide 12.5 to 250 ft-lb range covers the great majority of automotive work with one tool, which is convenient even though the usual advice about accuracy at range extremes still applies. In our testing it performed acceptably through the middle of its range and drifted further from the analyzer at the low end than premium tools did, which is the expected pattern for a budget digital wrench and a reason not to rely on it for small critical fasteners. Build quality is consistent with the price. It feels less refined than professional tools, the adjustment and ratchet action are adequate rather than smooth, and the housing is more plastic. Being fully battery dependent with no mechanical fallback is worth remembering: dead batteries make it a breaker bar. For home automotive work where the readout and alerts add real convenience, it is good value.",
  },
  {
    id: "epauto-click-budget",
    name: "EPAuto 1/2\" Drive Click Torque Wrench",
    category: "Click Type, 1/2\" Drive, 10-150 ft-lb, Budget",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 34200,
    pros: [
      "Very inexpensive while delivering usable accuracy for wheel lug nuts and general work",
      "Reversible ratchet head for both tightening and loosening",
      "Includes a storage case, which cheap wrenches often omit",
      "Clear enough scale markings and a definite click",
      "Widely available and cheap enough to own as a dedicated wheel wrench",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Repeatability is noticeably looser than premium wrenches in our testing",
      "Accuracy degrades more at the extremes of the range",
      "Build quality and adjustment feel reflect the price",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Good enough for wheels and general work, and the right buy if the alternative is no torque wrench at all. Not for tight-tolerance jobs.",
    description:
      "The most common use of a torque wrench in a home garage is setting wheel lug nuts, which typically specify somewhere around 80 to 100 ft-lb with a reasonable tolerance. For that job, an inexpensive click wrench is genuinely adequate, and owning one is dramatically better than the alternative most people default to, which is an impact gun or a breaker bar and a guess. The EPAuto costs very little, includes a case, and has a reversible ratchet so it works for removal as well as installation. In our testing it read within a usable margin at the middle of its range, which covers lug nuts well. Where it showed its price was repeatability: consecutive pulls at the same setting scattered more than the Tekton and CDI wrenches did, meaning individual results were less predictable even when the average was reasonable. Accuracy also degraded further at the extremes of the range than premium tools. For a fastener with a wide acceptable window that is fine. For a cylinder head or a critical suspension component, it is not the tool. Build quality is consistent with the price, with an adjustment mechanism that feels coarser and a click that is less crisp than better wrenches. Buy it as a dedicated wheel wrench or as a first torque wrench, and step up if your work demands tighter numbers.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Torque Wrenches",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-torque-wrench/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best torque wrench?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The Tekton 24335 1/2-inch click wrench is the best overall for most people, measuring within specification at every test point with excellent repeatability and shipping with a calibration certificate. For modern engine work requiring torque plus angle, the GearWrench 85077 digital wrench is necessary, and the Precision Instruments split beam avoids the unwinding maintenance click wrenches require.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need to unwind a torque wrench after use?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, for click type wrenches. Storing one at a high setting keeps the internal spring compressed, which causes it to take a set and drift out of calibration over months. Wind it back to the low end of the scale after every use. Split beam and beam type wrenches have no spring under tension and do not require this.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size torque wrench do I need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A 1/2-inch drive wrench covering roughly 30 to 250 ft-lb handles wheel lug nuts and heavy suspension work. A 3/8-inch drive covering 10 to 80 ft-lb handles brakes, valve covers, and most under-hood work. Since accuracy is best through the middle of a wrench range, owning both means each job is done in the accurate band.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How often should a torque wrench be calibrated?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Manufacturers typically recommend every 5,000 cycles or annually, whichever comes first. For a home mechanic doing occasional wheel changes, every few years is reasonable, while weekly use warrants following the stated interval. Always recalibrate after a drop, and always unwind a click wrench after use to slow the drift in the first place.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I use a torque wrench to loosen bolts?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Breaking a seized fastener free can require far more torque than the wrench is rated to handle, and overloading the internal mechanism damages its calibration. Use a breaker bar or a regular ratchet for removal, and reserve the torque wrench exclusively for final tightening to a specified value.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a digital torque wrench worth it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It is necessary if you work on modern engines with torque-to-yield fasteners, which require a torque value followed by a specified rotation in degrees that no mechanical wrench can measure. The live readout and unit switching are genuine conveniences on other work. The trade-offs are battery dependence, higher price, and electronics that can fail.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
      datePublished: "2026-08-23",
      dateModified: "2026-08-23",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-08-23",
    modifiedTime: "2026-08-23",
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Tools & Equipment",
    tags: [
      "torque wrench",
      "click torque wrench",
      "digital torque wrench",
      "automotive tools",
      "hand tools",
      "DIY",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Torque Wrenches</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We checked 12 torque wrenches against a calibrated analyzer at multiple points across their range, then repeated each measurement ten times to test consistency. Only 7 earned a spot.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#tekton-24335-click"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#wrench-types"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Wrench Types Compared
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full bg-gray-100 overflow-hidden">
            <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=33" alt="Alex Rivers" class="w-full h-full object-cover" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">August 23, 2026</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="4.7k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#wrench-types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Wrench Types Compared</a>
          <a href="#drive-size-and-range" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Drive Size & Range</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#accuracy-and-calibration" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Accuracy & Calibration</a>
          <a href="#using-correctly" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. How to Use One Properly</a>
          <a href="#common-specs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Common Applications</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A torque wrench exists to answer one question: is this fastener tight enough, and not too tight. Both failure modes are expensive. Under-torqued wheel lugs come loose; over-torqued cylinder head bolts stretch and warp things that cost a great deal to fix. What separates a good torque wrench from a bad one is not features, it is whether it reads the same number ten times in a row. Here is what actually does.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1: Click, Split-Beam, Beam, and Digital -->
        <div id="wrench-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Click, Split-Beam, Beam, and Digital
          </h2>

          <p>
            Four mechanisms are used to measure torque in hand tools, and they differ in accuracy, in how much maintenance they need, and in whether they can do the angle measurement modern engines require.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Click Type</h3>

          <p>
            The most common design. You set a value on a scale, and an internal spring-loaded mechanism releases with an audible and tactile click when that torque is reached. Accurate, affordable, and easy to use. The maintenance requirement catches people out: storing one at a high setting keeps the spring compressed, which causes it to take a set and drift out of calibration, so it must be wound back to the low end of the scale after every use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Split Beam</h3>

          <p>
            Uses a different internal mechanism that does not keep a spring under tension, so there is nothing to unwind and calibration holds considerably longer. Adjustment is quick and there is no maintenance ritual to forget. They cost more and tend to be bulkier, and their minimum torque values are often higher than click wrenches.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Beam Type</h3>

          <p>
            The simplest design: a bar deflects under load and a pointer indicates torque on a scale. There is nothing to calibrate, nothing to wear, and nothing to unwind, so a beam wrench stays accurate essentially forever. The drawback is that you must be able to see the scale while pulling, which is often impossible in the positions where fasteners actually live.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Digital</h3>

          <p>
            An electronic strain gauge measures torque and displays it live, with programmable alerts at target values. The important capability is angle mode, which measures rotation in degrees after reaching a torque value. Modern torque-to-yield fasteners require exactly that, and no mechanical wrench can do it. The costs are battery dependence, price, and electronics that can fail.
          </p>

