          <p>
            Choosing the right size is more consequential than choosing the right brand, because a wrench used outside its accurate band gives confidently wrong numbers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Accuracy Is Worst at the Extremes</h3>

          <p>
            Torque wrenches are most accurate through roughly the middle of their range, commonly quoted as 20 to 80 percent of full scale. A 10 to 150 ft-lb wrench set to 12 ft-lb is being used right at the bottom where its accuracy is poorest, and the reading may be well outside its stated specification. This is why one wrench cannot cover everything.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Match Drive Size to the Work</h3>

          <p>
            A 1/4-inch drive wrench covers inch-pound work: small electronics, interior trim, and delicate assemblies. A 3/8-inch drive covers roughly 10 to 80 ft-lb, which includes brakes, suspension, valve covers, and most under-hood work. A 1/2-inch drive covers roughly 30 to 250 ft-lb, which includes wheel lug nuts, axle nuts, and heavy suspension components.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Practical Two-Wrench Set</h3>

          <p>
