        <!-- SECTION 4: Accuracy, Repeatability, and Calibration -->
        <div id="accuracy-and-calibration" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Accuracy, Repeatability, and Calibration
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two properties determine whether a torque wrench is worth owning, and only one of them appears on the packaging.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Accuracy is how close a reading is to the true value.</strong> Consumer wrenches typically specify within four percent of reading, professional tools tighter. On a 100 ft-lb setting, four percent is four foot-pounds, which is well inside the tolerance of most fastener specifications.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Repeatability is whether it gives the same reading every time, and it matters more.</strong> A wrench that reads three percent high consistently is usable, because the error is predictable. A wrench that scatters across a ten-foot-pound band from pull to pull is not, even if the average is perfect, because any individual fastener could be anywhere in that band. This is what separated the good wrenches from the cheap ones in our testing far more clearly than headline accuracy did.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Calibration drifts with use and with storage.</strong> Manufacturers commonly recommend recalibration every 5,000 cycles or annually, whichever comes first. For a home mechanic doing seasonal wheel changes, annual recalibration is overkill; every few years is reasonable. For anyone using one weekly, follow the interval.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A calibration certificate is worth looking for.</strong> A certificate listing actual measured values from the individual tool test is meaningfully different from a printed statement of nominal specification. It shows the tool was tested rather than assumed to be within tolerance.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>You can sanity check a wrench at home.</strong> Clamp the drive in a vise, hang a known weight from the handle at a measured distance from the drive center, and compare the resulting torque against the setting where it clicks. Torque equals weight multiplied by distance, so 25 pounds at two feet is 50 ft-lb. It is not a substitute for professional calibration, and it will catch a wrench that has gone badly wrong.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Always Unwind a Click Wrench</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Storing a click wrench at a high setting keeps its internal spring compressed, and over months that spring takes a set and the tool drifts out of calibration. Wind it back to the low end of its scale, not to zero on wrenches whose manuals specify the lowest marked value, after every single use. This one habit does more to preserve accuracy than anything else you can do, and skipping it is why so many secondhand torque wrenches read wrong.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Using a Torque Wrench Correctly -->
        <div id="using-correctly" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Using a Torque Wrench Correctly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Technique introduces more error than most tools do. These are the details that separate a meaningful torque reading from a number that happens to appear on a scale.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Pull smoothly and stop at the click.</strong> A slow, steady pull gives the mechanism time to respond accurately. Jerking produces a spike that trips the click early, and continuing to pull after the click over-torques the fastener, sometimes badly. One click, then stop.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hold the handle at the marked grip point.</strong> Torque is force multiplied by distance from the fastener, and the wrench is calibrated assuming your hand is at the designated grip area. Gripping further out than that increases actual torque above the reading, and choking up reduces it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Never use a torque wrench to loosen fasteners.</strong> Breaking a seized bolt free can require far more torque than the wrench is rated for, and overloading the mechanism damages calibration. Use a breaker bar for removal and reserve the torque wrench for final tightening.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Threads must be clean and correctly prepared.</strong> Torque measures the resistance of the whole system, and friction from dirty, rusty, or damaged threads absorbs torque that never reaches clamping force. A fastener that reads 100 ft-lb through rusty threads may be holding far less than one at 100 ft-lb through clean ones.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Follow the specified lubrication condition.</strong> Torque specifications assume either dry or lubricated threads, and the difference is large. Applying oil or anti-seize to a fastener specified dry can produce substantially more clamping force at the same torque reading, which is a common way to stretch or snap bolts.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Respect the tightening sequence.</strong> On anything with multiple fasteners around a joint, wheels, cylinder heads, intake manifolds, tighten in the specified crossing pattern and in stages rather than fully tightening one at a time. Sequence distributes clamping load evenly and prevents warping.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not use extensions or adapters carelessly.</strong> A straight extension in line with the drive does not change torque, but a crow foot or an offset adapter that moves the fastener away from the drive centerline changes the effective lever length and therefore the actual torque applied. That requires a correction calculation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Common Applications and Torque Specs -->
        <div id="common-specs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Common Applications and Torque Specs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Torque specifications always come from the manufacturer for your specific vehicle or equipment, and the figures below are typical ranges to indicate what capacity you need rather than values to use.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wheel lug nuts.</strong> Commonly 80 to 100 ft-lb on passenger cars and higher on trucks, which is why a 1/2-inch drive wrench is the standard garage tool. Always tighten in a star pattern rather than around the circle, and retorque after the first drive following a wheel change, since seating can allow lugs to loosen slightly.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Brake caliper and bracket bolts.</strong> Typically in the 20 to 80 ft-lb range depending on the vehicle, which puts them squarely in 3/8-inch drive territory. These are safety-critical fasteners where both under and over-tightening cause real problems.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Spark plugs.</strong> Usually low, often 15 to 25 ft-lb, and highly sensitive to over-torquing because the threads are in aluminum on most modern engines. Stripping a spark plug thread in a cylinder head is an expensive repair, which makes this one of the strongest arguments for owning a properly ranged wrench.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Engine internals and cylinder heads.</strong> Frequently torque-to-yield, requiring a torque value followed by a specified rotation in degrees, and often a multi-stage sequence. This is the work that requires a digital wrench with angle mode, and it is the least forgiving of guesswork.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Bicycle and motorcycle components.</strong> Usually specified in inch-pounds or newton-metres and often very low, particularly on carbon fiber parts where over-torquing causes catastrophic failure. A 1/4-inch drive inch-pound wrench is the correct tool, and a 1/2-inch wrench is useless here.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Watch the units.</strong> One foot-pound equals twelve inch-pounds, and confusing the two produces errors of a factor of twelve in either direction. Newton-metres are common on European and Japanese equipment, and one foot-pound is roughly 1.36 newton-metres. Digital wrenches that switch units remove this hazard entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

