          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best torque wrench?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Tekton 24335 1/2-inch click wrench is the best overall for most people, measuring within specification at every test point with excellent repeatability and shipping with a calibration certificate. For modern engine work requiring torque plus angle, the GearWrench 85077 digital wrench is necessary, and the Precision Instruments split beam avoids the unwinding maintenance click wrenches require.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to unwind it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for click type wrenches. Storing one at a high setting keeps the internal spring compressed, which causes it to take a set and drift out of calibration over months. Wind it back to the low end of the scale after every use. Split beam and beam type wrenches have no spring under tension and do not require this.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size torque wrench do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 1/2-inch drive wrench covering roughly 30 to 250 ft-lb handles wheel lug nuts and heavy suspension work. A 3/8-inch drive covering 10 to 80 ft-lb handles brakes, valve covers, and most under-hood work. Since accuracy is best through the middle of a wrench range, owning both means each job is done in the accurate band.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should it be calibrated?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Manufacturers typically recommend every 5,000 cycles or annually, whichever comes first. For a home mechanic doing occasional wheel changes, every few years is reasonable, while weekly use warrants following the stated interval. Always recalibrate after a drop, and always unwind a click wrench after use to slow the drift in the first place.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use it to loosen bolts?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Breaking a seized fastener free can require far more torque than the wrench is rated to handle, and overloading the internal mechanism damages its calibration. Use a breaker bar or a regular ratchet for removal, and reserve the torque wrench exclusively for final tightening to a specified value.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a digital torque wrench worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is necessary if you work on modern engines with torque-to-yield fasteners, which require a torque value followed by a specified rotation in degrees that no mechanical wrench can measure. The live readout and unit switching are genuine conveniences on other work. The trade-offs are battery dependence, higher price, and electronics that can fail.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Torque Wrench Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Torque wrench errors mostly come from technique and storage rather than from the tool being inaccurate out of the box.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing a Click Wrench at a High Setting</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Leaving the spring compressed for months causes it to take a set, and the tool quietly drifts out of calibration while still clicking confidently. Wind it back to the low end of its scale after every use. This is the single most common reason a torque wrench that was accurate when new no longer is.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Continuing to Pull After the Click</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The click is the signal that you have reached the set value, and any further movement over-torques the fastener beyond it. On a critical fastener that can mean a stretched bolt or a stripped thread. One click and stop, and if you are unsure whether you felt it, back off and repeat rather than pulling harder.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Wrench at the Bottom of Its Range</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Torque wrenches are most accurate through roughly the middle of their range, and a 10 to 150 ft-lb wrench set to 12 ft-lb is being used exactly where it is least reliable. Owning a second, smaller wrench for lighter work keeps every job inside the accurate band and costs less than one bad repair.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Dry or Lubricated Specification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Torque specifications assume a particular thread condition, and applying oil or anti-seize to a fastener specified dry substantially reduces friction, which means far more clamping force at the same reading. That is how bolts get stretched and snapped while the wrench says the number was correct.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

